### Option 1: Standard Poisson Problem (Dirichlet boundary)
This is the most common starting point for finite element/PDE papers.

```latex
boundary $\partial \Omega$. We consider the following Poisson model problem:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:model}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
    -\Delta u &= f \quad \text{in } \Omega,\\
            u &= 0 \quad \text{on } \partial \Omega,
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $f \in L^2(\Omega)$ is a given source term and $\Delta$ denotes the Laplace operator. 
```

### Option 2: General Elliptic Problem with Mixed Boundaries
If your paper deals with Dirichlet and Neumann boundary conditions and a diffusion tensor.

```latex
boundary $\partial \Omega$. We assume that $\partial \Omega$ is partitioned into two disjoint, measurable parts $\Gamma_D$ and $\Gamma_N$ such that $\partial \Omega = \overline{\Gamma}_D \cup \overline{\Gamma}_N$ and $|\Gamma_D| > 0$. We consider the following second-order elliptic boundary value problem:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:model}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
    -\nabla \cdot (A \nabla u) &= f \quad \text{in } \Omega,\\
                             u &= 0 \quad \text{on } \Gamma_D,\\
    (A \nabla u) \cdot \mathbf{n} &= g \quad \text{on } \Gamma_N,
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $n$ is the unit outward normal vector to $\partial \Omega$, $f \in L^2(\Omega)$, and $A(\mathbf{x})$ is a uniformly positive definite diffusion tensor.
```

### Option 3: Time-Dependent (Parabolic / Heat Equation)
If your paper focuses on time-evolution problems.

```latex
boundary $\partial \Omega$. For a given final time $T > 0$, we consider the following parabolic initial-boundary value problem:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:model}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
    \partial_t u - \Delta u &= f \quad \text{in } \Omega \times (0, T],\\
                          u &= 0 \quad \text{on } \partial \Omega \times (0, T],\\
                    u(\cdot, 0) &= u_0 \quad \text{in } \Omega,
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $f \in L^2(0,T; L^2(\Omega))$ is the source term and $u_0 \in L^2(\Omega)$ is the initial data.
```

